[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## How the harness keeps the comparison honest
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The library's only output is what it `printf`s, and it carries mutable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m file-scope state (`static house_t the_house`) that every call advances. Each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `.so` has its **own** copy of that state, so a "step" delivers the same call to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C and then to Rust while holding a global lock, and compares the captured bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because every step is atomic, both libraries observe the identical global call
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sequence and their states stay in lockstep for the whole run.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `errno` is treated as a second observable: `parse_val` writes it, and the value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m left behind after `driver` returns is compared between C and Rust on every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error-path test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Results
